/P 111 0 R
/T (Li, Y., et al. \(2023\) 'Emotion-Aware Music Recommendation via Multimodal Fusion', ACM Multimedia Conference Proceedings. This work proposes a system that fuses audio features with user physiological signals to recommend music. While achieving higher personalization, the system requires specialized hardware for physiological sensing. The proposed approach in this project simplifies the pipeline to camera-only input, making it more accessible.  Panda, R., Malheiro, R. M., and Paiva, R. P. \(2022\) 'Music Mood  Classification and Recommendation: A Survey', Expert Systems with  Applications, Vol.185. This paper explores audio feature extraction such as  MFCC, chroma, and spectral contrast for automatic music mood tagging. The  authors demonstrate that emotion labels aligned with Russell's circumplex  model of affect translate naturally to music genre categories. This finding  underpins our emotion-to-genre mapping strategy.  Picard, R. W. \(2019\) 'Affective Computing: From Laughter to IEEE', IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing. This paper provides foundational theory for emotion-aware computing systems. The paper outlines the six universal facial expressions as proposed by Ekman, namely happiness, sadness, fear, disgust, anger, and surprise, forming the theoretical basis for the seven-class emotion model used in this project.  Rahman, A., et al. \(2020\) 'Benchmarking Face Detection Algorithms for Real- Time Applications', Journal of Real-Time Image Processing. This work  benchmarks several face detection algorithms under varying lighting and  occlusion conditions. Haar Cascade classifiers are found to offer the best  speed-accuracy tradeoff for real-time applications on standard CPUs,  justifying its use in this project for the face detection pipeline.  Sandler, M., Howard, A., Zhu, M., Zhmoginov, A., and Chen, L. \(2018\)  'MobileNetV2: Inverted Residuals and Linear Bottlenecks', Proceedings of )
/E (Li, Y., et al. \(2023\) 'Emotion-Aware Music Recommendation via Multimodal Fusion', ACM Multimedia Conference Proceedings. This work proposes a system that fuses audio features with user physiological signals to recommend music. While achieving higher personalization, the system requires specialized hardware for physiological sensing. The proposed approach in this project simplifies the pipeline to camera-only input, making it more accessible.  Panda, R., Malheiro, R. M., and Paiva, R. P. \(2022\) 'Music Mood  Classification and Recommendation: A Survey', Expert Systems with  Applications, Vol.185. This paper explores audio feature extraction such as  MFCC, chroma, and spectral contrast for automatic music mood tagging. The  authors demonstrate that emotion labels aligned with Russell's circumplex  model of affect translate naturally to music genre categories. This finding  underpins our emotion-to-genre mapping strategy.  Picard, R. W. \(2019\) 'Affective Computing: From Laughter to IEEE', IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing. This paper provides foundational theory for emotion-aware computing systems. The paper outlines the six universal facial expressions as proposed by Ekman, namely happiness, sadness, fear, disgust, anger, and surprise, forming the theoretical basis for the seven-class emotion model used in this project.  Rahman, A., et al. \(2020\) 'Benchmarking Face Detection Algorithms for Real- Time Applications', Journal of Real-Time Image Processing. This work  benchmarks several face detection algorithms under varying lighting and  occlusion conditions. Haar Cascade classifiers are found to offer the best  speed-accuracy tradeoff for real-time applications on standard CPUs,  justifying its use in this project for the face detection pipeline.  Sandler, M., Howard, A., Zhu, M., Zhmoginov, A., and Chen, L. \(2018\)  'MobileNetV2: Inverted Residuals and Linear Bottlenecks', Proceedings of )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
